in the run-up to World War 2 France  spent almost a decade building the  famous Majin o-line 450 miles of  concrete bunkers and weapon  installations protected the country from  an impending invasion and it was  heralded as a work of military genius  because if everything went in Frances  favor the plan was simple hold off  German forces long enough to mobilize a  massive counter-attack but of course  things did not go in Frances favor  Germany got past this modern great wall  the simplest way in knew how by walking  around it based on Germany's strategy  during World War one French officials  assumed the attackers would avoid the  rough terrain of the Ardennes Forest but  Germany revised its battle plans  sweeping through the weak point in the  art den rendering the southern defences  useless the Majah no line has since  become a parable for grandiose efforts  that offered no real security I'm  reminded of the marginal line every time  I play they are billions developed by  Spanish studio new montien games and  released in early access two months ago  this RTS punishes anyone who refuses to  adapt if you don't learn from your  mistakes you'll likely die they are  billion seems initially simple but it  hides a multitude of layers  it takes place in a procedurally  generated steampunk post-apocalypse you  have to build a new colony expand it and  defend it from increasingly large waves  of zombies this may sound like a  combination of tired tropes but it's  actually remarkable how well they all  come together it's one of those rare  games where every detail is essential to  its personality and design the  procedural generation for instance  forces you into a survivors mindset in  one match you can force the zombies into  a choke point between the cliffs and a  nearby lake in the next you have to  expand your colony much sooner to take  advantage of an iron deposit for your  snipers weapons the unpredictable  placement of forests coastlines and  zombie villages forces you not to master  specific Maps but the actual rules of  the wasteland it's not about memorizing  the same terrain over and over again  it's about learning resource management  and troop placement so you can make the  best of each random landscape as for the  steampunk trappings these two play a  pivotal role in the mechanics of the  game yes the cel-shaded valves and steam  whistles are vibrant and gorgeous but  they also solve the perennial challenge  of RTS base construction some games  solve this with limited build plots  others expand outward from something  like a Construction Yard  they are billions however lets you snake  your base around lakes through canyons  and into the far corners of each map you  accomplish this with Tesla coils build  one and it provides energy to nearby  farms turrets and other Tesla coils by  the late game stages you'll likely have  dozens of these towers strung throughout  the landscape powering your  post-apocalyptic colony in its own  clever way they are billions let's it's  setting drive its gameplay and of course  this also rings true for the game's  undead horde we've seen zombies and  dozens if not hundreds of games over the  past 25 years they've shambled through  every genre on every platform we've  owned and as the zombie trope  oversaturated games as well as TV and  film it became an ironic commentary on  itself a lifeless husk limping forward  with no end in sight but the zombie  stories that stuck with us were the ones  that understood why zombies could be  compelling to begin with Resident Evil  used them to constantly morph your  expectations of Spencer mansion  left4dead which actually turns ten this  year use them to create cooperative  survival scenarios Shaun of the Dead  satirized the simultaneous terror and  ridiculousness of the undead masses they  are billions joins the pantheon of  memorable zombie tales in fact to point  to a recent entry in the genre they are  billions does World War Z better than  World War Z in new mantheon games RTS  zombies swarm your defenses in tidal  waves coming from random directions  pouring through choke points and  overwhelming your carefully prepared  defenses on its website new man team  claims that can have 20,000 zombies on  screen at one time and after 40 hours  with this game I can confirm that's true  with this kind of scale they are  billions shows just how helpless your  colony can be of course zombies aren't  just scary because of their numbers but  also how they multiply here to new  mantheon uses clever systems to heighten  the tension in almost every other RTS  one basic grunt unit won't usually lead  to game over and they are billions  however that one basic zombie can infect  a small building spawn a few more  friends infect two more buildings and so  on until a horde has formed behind your  walls in a matter of seconds these  massive swarms of enemies are all the  more terrifying when you realize just  how dangerous each individual unit can  be all of this adds up to a game with an  extremely strong identity it's a  steampunk post-apocalypse zombie RTS and  all of these components are essential in  creating an exhilarating experience it's  a deep strategy game it's a terrifying  zombie entry it uses procedural  generation to its advantage and even now  I can't wait to play more because not  only is they are billions thrilling but  it's one of the best realtime strategy  games to release in the last decade this  is high praise contrary to recent  refrain the genre has not been dying and  it certainly is not dead  this sentiment ignores games such as  total war Warhammer to homeworld deserts  of carrot or the exceptional off world  trading company whether through  large-scale battles or economic  management these titles offer fresh  takes on the arty  legacy and what a legacy that is some of  the best PC games have sprung from this  lineage we have franchises such as  command &amp; Conquer Age of Empires and  total war  not to mention licensed games such as  Star Wars Empire at war or battle for  middle-earth and its sequel and of  course we have Warcraft orcs and humans  the game that would spark a franchise  and become the breakthrough hit for one  of today's juggernaut developers it was  Warcraft competition with command &amp;  Conquer that sparked the RTS boom of the  late 90s while Westwood Studios series  emphasized military action blizzard  pursued story and characterization this  rivalry gave birth to Starcraft in 1998  another Blizzard project that proves  successful bringing the studio's refined  RTS formula to a sci-fi setting but it  was Starcraft 2 in 2010 that truly  refined that formula it took everything  that made its predecessor excellent  except local land capabilities and  amplified it even more its factions have  distinct identities and play styles it  tells an engaging story with memorable  characters and like many Blizzard games  its systems are easy enough to learn but  very difficult to master it's one of the  best realtime strategy games because the  individual parts of its identity are all  essential to its overall design they are  billions continues that innovative  legacy and as of now 500,000 people have  tried it it may only be in early access  but it is one of the best realtime  strategy games in years and one of the  best zombie games to date if you've been  craving an intelligent RTS that reveals  new depths each time you play they are  billions as your game if you've been  craving a great zombie game in a  landscape where they are oversaturated  they are billions deserve your time  however before we go I do have one piece  of advice always remember the majah no  line  [Music]
 

